  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p><img src=\"assets\\st0068_c49572aa2c5b.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 205px;\" /></p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0068_57aa5b173ee1.png\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 199px;\" /></p><p>In the first circuit diagram, the diodes&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;are forward biased and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is reverse biased(Hence, no current through it). So the circuit is simplified to the second diagram.</p><p>Hence, the equivalent resistance is&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>20</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math></p><p>Using Ohm&#39;s law, the current is&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>10</mn><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mn>20</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>30</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math></p></blockquote>"
  }
